WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.375
Convexity says that a local minimum is globally optimal within a convex domain. It does not

00:00:06.375 --> 00:00:12.550
guarantee that a particular iterative method reaches that minimum at any chosen learning rate. The shape

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:16.410
of the objective determines how aggressive an update can be.

00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:23.173
This quadratic is strictly convex. The curvature a controls how quickly the slope changes as the

00:00:23.173 --> 00:00:23.953
input moves.

00:00:24.467 --> 00:00:30.075
The gradient tells us a direction at one point. The second derivative tells us how aggressively

00:00:30.075 --> 00:00:31.477
the gradient changes nearby.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.184
Will a fixed learning rate remain safe as the curve gets steeper? Pause here and make

00:00:37.184 --> 00:00:38.480
a prediction before continuing.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:47.526
Keeping the learning rate fixed while increasing curvature makes the effective step more aggressive. For our

00:00:47.526 --> 00:00:52.480
starting inputs, Curvature: 2. Updated weight: 0.4. Error multiplier: 0.2.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.984
Strict contraction gives convergence for this example. Convexity alone does not make every learning rate safe;

00:00:58.984 --> 00:01:02.350
a must be below five for this fixed rate.

00:01:02.867 --> 00:01:09.117
State curvature or smoothness assumptions when using a convergence claim, and handle constraints explicitly.

